72
00:03:37.755 --> 00:03:40.095
And so, where does this retina come from?

73
00:03:42.615 --> 00:03:44.585
Do you remember this first vesicle?

74
00:03:44.625 --> 00:03:47.355
this small sphere that emerges, which we call the

75
00:03:47.395 --> 00:03:48.855
primary optic vesicle,

76
00:03:50.705 --> 00:03:53.145
which will make a movement like this,

77
00:03:53.185 --> 00:03:54.825
you see, like a calyx, a cup.

78
00:03:57.665 --> 00:03:59.905
It is made of two layers, like this,

79
00:03:59.945 --> 00:04:02.395
you see, an inner layer, an outer layer.

80
00:04:02.885 --> 00:04:05.365
And this is where you understand what is called a

81
00:04:05.405 --> 00:04:06.305
retinal detachment.

82
00:04:06.985 --> 00:04:08.573
We can clearly see these two layers.

83
00:04:10.025 --> 00:04:12.595
Well, when people have a retinal detachment,

84
00:04:12.635 --> 00:04:15.435
it is this layer that expands, it is this space

85
00:04:15.475 --> 00:04:17.915
between the inner leaflet and the outer leaflet of

86
00:04:17.955 --> 00:04:18.805
the retina.

87
00:04:20.156 --> 00:04:22.426
There will be another very thin layer here,

88
00:04:22.466 --> 00:04:24.306
which will be very important later,

89
00:04:24.346 --> 00:04:26.546
